B=[
L(226,'Electronics and RF','What is impedance matching?','Impedance matching controls reflection or power/signal transfer by making source, line and load impedances satisfy the intended frequency-dependent condition.','On a transmission line, matching Z_L to characteristic impedance makes the load reflection coefficient zero at the design frequency. For a source/load network, conjugate matching can maximize delivered power under stated assumptions, while voltage measurement often prefers high input impedance instead. Matching can use transformers, reactive networks or distributed structures and is bandwidth-, loss- and tolerance-limited. The objective must be stated before saying impedances should be equal.', ['Linear steady-state network at stated frequency','Reference impedance and matching objective defined'],['Saying every circuit needs equal impedances','Ignoring bandwidth and reactive components'],['Reflection coefficient?','Conjugate match?','Why scope input is high?','How measure S11?','What limits bandwidth?']),
L(227,'Electromagnetic Theory','What are boundary conditions for electric fields?','Boundary conditions follow Maxwell equations: tangential E jumps with time-varying magnetic surface effects as appropriate, while normal D changes by free surface charge; electrostatic tangential E is continuous.','A shrinking loop across an electrostatic interface gives n×(E₂−E₁)=0. A pillbox gives n·(D₂−D₁)=σ_free in macroscopic media. Normal E therefore depends on permittivity and surface charge. At a perfect conductor in electrostatic equilibrium, interior E is zero, the surface is equipotential and external tangential E vanishes; normal external field is tied to surface charge. Dynamic interfaces require the full Maxwell boundary relations.', ['Interface normal and macroscopic/microscopic fields defined','Electrostatic simplifications used only where stated'],['Saying all E components are continuous','Using D boundary condition with total charge without convention'],['Derive with loop.','Derive with pillbox.','Dielectric interface?','Perfect conductor?','What changes dynamically?']),
L(228,'Project Ownership','What is your independent contribution to the project?','State only work personally completed, separating observed, assisted, supervised and independently repeatable tasks; unknown or planned contribution remains explicitly pending.','I would answer with specific actions: literature or protocol work, sample preparation, instrument operation, code, analysis, decisions, troubleshooting and documentation that I actually performed. I would name guidance from my supervisor and facility staff, then identify one judgement I made and what I can safely repeat under approved procedure. If the project is early-stage, a narrow truthful contribution is stronger than group credit. The Mission Viva ledger must be filled with real facts before this answer is personalized.', ['Actual notebook/contribution records available','Facility authorization distinguished from competence'],['Using “we” to hide personal role','Claiming independence after observing once'],['Which decision was yours?','What required supervision?','What can you repeat?','Where documented?','What did you learn?']),
L(229,'Radiation Protection','Why is shielding material-dependent?','Shielding depends on radiation type and energy because interaction probabilities, secondary radiation, density, composition and geometry differ across materials.','Charged particles, photons and neutrons lose energy through different mechanisms. High-Z material may attenuate photons efficiently but can increase bremsstrahlung for energetic beta particles; hydrogen-rich materials can slow neutrons while capture products also need consideration. Real design includes spectrum, buildup, gaps, activation, heat and dose geometry. Therefore no material is a universal shield, and facility shielding must be designed by qualified personnel under current standards.', ['Conceptual comparison with radiation spectrum specified','No operational shielding design inferred'],['Naming lead as universal shield','Ignoring secondary radiation and geometry'],['Beta shielding issue?','Why hydrogen for neutrons?','What is buildup?','How energy matters?','Who verifies design?']),
L(230,'Spacecraft Thermal Engineering','Why are thermal controls needed on spacecraft?','Spacecraft need thermal control because vacuum removes convection while solar, planetary, internal and eclipse loads vary; components must remain within survival and performance temperatures.','Heat moves by conduction through structure and radiation between surfaces and space. Absorptivity, emissivity, view factors, internal dissipation, orbit/attitude and transient heat capacity determine temperatures. Passive coatings, insulation, radiators and heat paths can be combined with heaters or active transport. “Space is cold” does not guarantee cooling, and vacuum does not prevent radiation. A credible answer starts with node energy balances and validated thermal-vacuum correlation.', ['Orbit/attitude and component limits specified','Radiative and conductive paths modelled'],['Saying vacuum makes a spacecraft freeze','Ignoring internal power and eclipse transients'],['Why no convection?','Role of emissivity?','What is MLI?','Why heaters?','How validate model?']),
L(231,'Research Integrity','How do you avoid confirmation bias?','Predefine hypotheses, controls, inclusion and decision criteria; preserve all data; use blinding/randomization where possible; seek alternatives and tests that could falsify the preferred explanation.','I would distinguish exploratory from confirmatory analysis and avoid changing criteria silently after seeing results. Negative and contradictory runs remain in the record. Independent replication, code review, held-out data, competing models and a planned discriminating experiment reduce motivated interpretation. Bias cannot be eliminated by confidence alone; transparent methods, provenance and criticism create checks.', ['Testable competing explanations exist','Raw data and analysis history preserved'],['Claiming objectivity removes bias','Discarding inconvenient runs as outliers without criteria'],['What would falsify claim?','Why preregister?','How blind experiment?','Exploratory versus confirmatory?','How handle contradiction?']),
L(232,'Electromagnetic Theory','What happens at a conductor surface?','In electrostatic equilibrium the conductor interior field is zero, the surface is equipotential, tangential external E vanishes and surface charge supports the normal field; finite-frequency conductors require skin depth and surface-current physics.','Free charges move until no tangential force remains. A Gaussian pillbox relates the normal field or D discontinuity to surface charge. Just outside an ideal vacuum conductor E_n=σ/ε₀. Surface curvature changes charge density, and cavities depend on enclosed charge and boundary conditions. In real finite-conductivity AC fields, penetration, loss and current occur over a skin depth, so the ideal electrostatic statement must not be generalized.', ['Electrostatic equilibrium and ideal conductor for simple result','Surface normal and surrounding medium specified'],['Saying fields are always zero near conductors','Ignoring finite-frequency penetration'],['Why tangential E zero?','Field inside cavity?','Curvature effect?','What is skin depth?','How surface current arises?']),
L(233,'Thermodynamics','Why is enthalpy useful for constant-pressure processes?','Enthalpy H=U+PV packages internal energy and pressure–volume displacement; for a closed system with only PV work at constant pressure, heat added equals ΔH.','From dU=δQ−P_ext dV, a quasistatic constant-pressure process with only PV work gives δQ=dU+P dV=dH. In open steady-flow systems, h naturally appears with flow work in the energy balance. The equality Q_p=ΔH fails with non-PV work, varying external conditions or unaccounted kinetic/potential changes. Enthalpy is a state function; heat remains path dependent.', ['Closed system, constant pressure and only PV work for Qp relation','Equilibrium states and sign convention defined'],['Calling enthalpy heat content universally','Using Q=ΔH with electrical/shaft work unaccounted'],['Derive dH.','Open-system role?','Ideal-gas enthalpy?','When Qp fails?','Why state function?']),
L(234,'Thermodynamics','Why does free energy decrease spontaneously at fixed T and P?','For a closed system at fixed reservoir temperature and pressure with only PV work, spontaneous change gives ΔG≤0; equality marks equilibrium under those constraints.','Combining first and second laws for system plus reservoirs gives the Gibbs criterion when temperature and pressure are controlled and composition/reaction variables can change. G decreases because total entropy of the isolated composite increases, not because every system always minimizes G. Other constraints use U, H or F, and ΔG says direction/available non-PV work, not rate. Kinetic barriers can keep a metastable state for a long time.', ['Closed system at fixed T and P','Only specified work modes and equilibrium endpoints'],['Saying G always decreases under any conditions','Equating negative ΔG with fast reaction'],['Derive from entropy.','What potential at fixed T,V?','What is metastability?','Equilibrium criterion?','How chemical potential enters?']),
L(235,'Phase Transitions','What is a phase transition order parameter?','An order parameter is a quantity that distinguishes phases and changes characteristically across a transition, often reflecting broken symmetry; it must be chosen for the system.','Examples include magnetization in a ferromagnet or density difference near a liquid–gas critical point. In a continuous transition it may approach zero continuously, while a first-order transition can jump. It is not always directly measurable and can be multi-component or topological. Landau theory expands free energy in an assumed order parameter and symmetry, but fluctuations and dimensionality can invalidate mean-field predictions.', ['Phases and symmetry/transition class defined','Thermodynamic limit or finite-size qualification stated'],['Calling any measured property an order parameter','Assuming every transition breaks symmetry conventionally'],['Magnet example?','First versus second order?','What is conjugate field?','Why fluctuations matter?','Topological transition?']),
L(236,'Statistical Mechanics','What is chemical potential for a classical ideal gas?','Chemical potential is the free-energy change per added particle and, for a dilute classical ideal gas, depends logarithmically on density and thermal wavelength, with internal-state factors as defined.','For a monatomic ideal gas, μ=kT ln(nλ_T³/g) under a common convention, where λ_T=h/√(2πmkT) and g represents internal degeneracy. In the classical regime nλ_T³/g≪1, μ is negative relative to the chosen single-particle energy zero. Chemical potential drives particle exchange/diffusion and equalizes at equilibrium. Energy-zero and standard-state conventions must be stated.', ['Dilute noninteracting classical gas','Thermal wavelength/internal degeneracy convention defined'],['Treating μ as energy per particle','Quoting formula outside classical regime'],['Derive from partition function.','Why negative?','What equalizes?','Quantum-gas change?','How energy zero affects μ?']),
L(237,'Nuclear and Radiation Physics','Why are gamma rays not deflected by electric fields?','Gamma rays are electrically neutral photons, so they do not experience the Lorentz force q(E+v×B) that bends charged particles.','Photons can still interact with matter through photoelectric absorption, Compton scattering, pair production and fields indirectly through matter or gravity. Their momentum direction can change in scattering, and refractive effects may be described in media, but a static electric field does not curve a free gamma ray as it would an electron. Neutrality does not mean non-interacting or harmless.', ['Free photon in classical external electromagnetic field','Matter interactions considered separately'],['Saying gamma rays never change direction','Equating neutrality with no biological interaction'],['How gamma interacts?','Why beta deflects?','What about magnetic field?','Photon momentum?','Pair production condition?']),
L(238,'Laboratory Safety Culture','What is your relationship with safety in a laboratory?','Safety is part of scientific quality: I work within authorization, understand hazards, use approved procedures and controls, document deviations, and stop/ask when conditions are unclear.','I would review risk assessment and training before work, verify sample/reagent/equipment identity, use engineering controls and PPE as specified, maintain housekeeping and records, and report incidents or near misses. I would not improvise radiation, high-voltage, pressure, vacuum, laser, cryogenic or chemical controls. Hierarchy or schedule does not override a mandatory procedure. My exact project hazards must remain unknown until recorded from the real laboratory process.', ['Institutional procedures and competent supervision available','Actual hazards identified before work'],['Describing safety as PPE only','Claiming willingness to bypass procedure for schedule'],['What if procedure unclear?','How report near miss?','When stop work?','Why document deviation?','What hazard applies to your project?']),
L(239,'Defence Materials','Why are materials important in defence systems?','Materials determine whether structures, sensors, optics, electronics and protection systems retain required properties under mechanical, thermal, chemical, electromagnetic and environmental constraints.','Selection can involve strength-to-weight, fatigue, fracture, corrosion, temperature, wear, dielectric/magnetic/optical response, radiation, manufacturability, repair and supply. A material property measured on a coupon is not system readiness: joints, coatings, defects, processing, variability and qualification matter. I would connect one public requirement to a measurable property and a representative test without speculating about restricted performance.', ['Public non-sensitive requirement selected','Service environment and failure criterion defined'],['Listing exotic materials without requirement','Equating highest strength with best system material'],['Which property?','How processing matters?','What environmental test?','Why variability?','How qualify joint/coating?']),
L(240,'Astrophysical Modelling','What assumptions would you state in an astrophysical model?','State geometry/symmetry, gravity and dynamics, equation of state, composition/opacity, equilibrium or time dependence, radiation/transport, initial/boundary conditions and how observables are generated.','The relevant list depends on the question: a stellar model may assume spherical hydrostatic equilibrium and specified transport; a galaxy model may assume axisymmetry or circular orbits; a cosmology model homogeneity/isotropy at large scales. I would distinguish physical approximations from numerical choices, identify parameter degeneracies and selection effects, and name an observable or limiting case that tests each dominant assumption.', ['Specific astrophysical question and observable defined','Approximation regime and data selection stated'],['Reciting assumptions with no consequence','Treating numerical convergence as physical validation'],['Which assumption dominates?','What observable tests it?','How boundary chosen?','What degeneracy?','What limiting case?']),
L(241,'Materials Research','Why are reproducibility and scale-up important in materials research?','Reproducibility tests whether a result survives independent preparation/measurement; scale-up tests whether process, uniformity, safety and performance persist when geometry, equipment or throughput changes.','A striking single sample can reflect local heterogeneity, uncontrolled processing or measurement artifact. Independent batches, positions, operators and instruments reveal variance. Scale changes heat/mass transfer, mixing, atmosphere, contamination, gradients and sampling, so multiplying quantities is insufficient. I would define acceptance criteria, process windows, yield and uncertainty, while distinguishing laboratory proof from manufacturable technology.', ['Material claim and preparation protocol defined','Independent batch and larger-scale conditions traceable'],['Calling repeated scans reproducibility','Assuming linear scale-up'],['Repeatability versus reproducibility?','How many batches?','Which gradient changes?','How define yield?','What pilot test?']),
L(242,'Project Motivation','Why did you choose this material or system?','Answer from real facts: connect the scientific question to a property or mechanism, evidence gap, feasible method and learning value; if material is unconfirmed, say so.','A defensible answer compares the chosen system with at least one alternative and explains why its structure, composition or response can test the project objective using available safe facilities. It should not say merely that the material is popular, easy or grade-friendly. The current Mission Viva material field is unknown, so the app cannot personalize this answer without invention. Once known, cite actual literature and supervisor-approved scope.', ['Actual material/system and objective confirmed','Alternative and facility constraints known'],['Inventing application or novelty','Choosing only because synthesis is easy'],['What property matters?','Which alternative?','What evidence gap?','Why feasible?','What result would change choice?']),
L(243,'Project Motivation','What scientific gap motivates the project?','Identify a specific unresolved comparison, mechanism, measurement limitation or condition from real literature—not a broad claim that the topic is unexplored.','I would state what is already established, what evidence is missing or contradictory, why that missing piece matters, and the observable this project can measure. The gap must fit the actual material, method and project duration. A literature gap is not automatically scientific importance, and no gap should be invented before the project system and sources are recorded. A good gap leads to a falsifiable objective and control.', ['Actual literature and material/system confirmed','Gap is tractable with available method'],['Calling any untested material combination a gap','Claiming “first” without search'],['What is known?','Which contradiction?','What observable?','Why tractable?','What result closes gap?']),
L(244,'Project Motivation','Why is this problem worth studying?','Explain the fundamental or practical consequence of resolving the actual question, tied to a measurable property and evidence—not a generic application promise.','Worth can come from understanding a mechanism, improving measurement confidence, comparing models, establishing reproducibility or informing a device/process decision. I would identify who or what decision benefits and the current uncertainty cost. For an early-stage project, learning a reliable synthesis–structure relation can be worthwhile even without a final application. The answer must use confirmed project facts and avoid selection-oriented exaggeration.', ['Actual objective and stakeholder/scientific context known','Expected evidence is achievable'],['Equating novelty with importance','Promising a device from sample characterization'],['Who benefits?','What decision changes?','What uncertainty now?','Fundamental or applied value?','What negative result teaches?']),
L(245,'Project Translation','What would make the project important beyond your laboratory?','Importance beyond the lab requires reproducible evidence, a property tied to external requirements, independent validation, scale/interface understanding and a user or scientific decision—not merely a possible application.','I would separate the present sample claim from future translation. The project gains broader value if another group can reproduce it, the measurement is traceable, performance is compared with a baseline, operating constraints and failure modes are known, and a next prototype or dataset addresses a real need. Until actual results exist, this remains a planned impact pathway rather than an achievement.', ['Actual project evidence and application requirement known','Independent validation pathway available'],['Calling publication or one graph broad impact','Claiming scalability without process evidence'],['What baseline?','Who validates?','Which requirement?','What scale-up issue?','What is next prototype?']),
L(246,'Project Synthesis','Which parameter most strongly affects the synthesis?','Do not guess: identify the parameter with the strongest mechanistic sensitivity and evidence from literature, controls or a designed series; otherwise state it is not yet known.','Candidates may include stoichiometry, pH, solvent, mixing, temperature, dwell, ramp/cooling, atmosphere or pressure. I would explain how the parameter affects reaction, nucleation, growth, phase, defects or contamination, then hold other variables controlled and define a measurable response. The actual route is unknown in the current ledger, so no parameter can be named honestly yet.', ['Actual synthesis route and chemistry recorded','Parameter range and response measurable'],['Naming temperature universally','Changing several parameters at once'],['Mechanism?','What range?','What control?','How measure response?','Could interaction dominate?']),
L(247,'Project Synthesis','How would precursor ratio change the result?','Precursor ratio can change stoichiometry, chemical potential, reaction pathway, defects and secondary phases, but the direction is system-specific and must be tested from actual chemistry.','I would distinguish nominal weighed ratio from incorporated composition because purity, volatility, solubility, reaction completeness and loss matter. A controlled ratio series with mass/composition checks, phase analysis and property measurement can reveal sensitivity. Excess precursor may compensate loss or create an impurity; no universal prediction is safe. The project precursors are currently unknown and must not be invented.', ['Actual precursors, valence/chemistry and route known','Other synthesis conditions controlled'],['Assuming weighed ratio equals final stoichiometry','Predicting phase direction without chemistry'],['How verify composition?','What impurity possible?','Why volatility matters?','Which ratio series?','What safety changes?']),
L(248,'Project Characterization','What does your main characterization technique actually measure?','Name the physical interaction, detector signal and calibrated quantity—not the final conclusion—and distinguish direct observable from model-derived output.','A strong answer traces stimulus to sample interaction, emitted/transmitted/scattered response, detector and processing. For XRD the detector records diffracted intensity versus angle; for microscopy it records selected electron/optical signals versus position; for spectroscopy it records response versus energy/wavelength. Phase, composition or property claims require assumptions, calibration, sampling and complementary evidence. The actual main technique is unknown in the ledger.', ['Actual technique and mode confirmed','Instrument geometry and calibration known'],['Saying the instrument directly measures “structure” or “truth”','Ignoring processing and sampling volume'],['What is stimulus?','What detector records?','Which output is derived?','What artifact dominates?','What can falsify interpretation?']),
L(249,'Project Characterization','What can this technique not establish?','State the method’s claim boundary from its interaction, sampling depth/volume, resolution, detection limit, model and artifacts; never answer “nothing” or “everything.”','Every technique leaves alternatives. XRD may miss amorphous/minor phases and does not prove composition or morphology; EDS does not reliably give oxidation state; XPS is surface-sensitive; microscopy is local and may be unrepresentative; optical edges are model-dependent. The exact limitation must match the actual mode and sample. I would name one complementary method chosen to address the most decision-critical gap.', ['Actual technique and claim specified','Sensitivity and sampling scale known'],['Giving a memorized limitation unrelated to mode','Assuming expensive technique is definitive'],['Detection limit?','Sampling depth?','Main artifact?','What complementary method?','Could nondetection prove absence?']),
L(250,'Project Characterization','Why do you need a complementary technique?','Different techniques measure different physical quantities and scales, so complementary evidence tests separate clauses and competing explanations of the project claim.','A structure–property claim may require periodic structure, composition, morphology, surface chemistry and functional response. I would choose methods with distinct sensitivities and artifacts, align samples and scales, and specify what disagreement would mean. More plots are not automatically stronger evidence; each technique must reduce a named uncertainty. Actual project techniques remain unknown until entered.', ['Scientific claim decomposed into measurable clauses','Technique sensitivities/artifacts understood'],['Collecting techniques for quantity rather than discrimination','Treating agreement as causation'],['Which claim clause?','How scales differ?','What if disagree?','What is cheapest next method?','Which uncertainty remains?'])]
